==> causal-layer: full  (5/5 of decision ticket 08)
  [x] 1. A causal-edge schema (assertion + evidence grade + confidence) in ubiquitous language.
        build ticket 17: twin/schema.py — an `influences` edge asserts sign, lag, a min/mode/max elasticity, an evidence grade 1-5 and an optional confidence, all validated on write. A degenerate triple is permitted and flagged rather than refused, so false precision is visible.
  [x] 2. Defined intervention + counterfactual semantics, incl. behaviour on structural-only paths.
        build ticket 81: tests/test_four_verbs.py::test_the_full_counterfactual_composes_abduction_action_and_prediction (build ticket 81). Abduction (`primitives.rewind`, build ticket 35), action (`Do` carried by `verbs.intervene`, build ticket 22) and prediction/fast-forward (`verbs.run`, build ticket 37) are called together off the identical abducted state — the two prior tests each composed only two of the three (`test_counterfactual_is_rewind_then_intervene`: abduction+action; `test_backtest_is_rewind_then_run`: abduction+prediction), which is what the capability file's own prior note meant by 'two thirds of a composition is not the composition'. Structural-only-path behaviour is stated and tested for this same composed (rewound + intervened) chain, not only for `propagate()` at rest: `do(cloud-compute)` — a component with zero `influences` edges of its own — produces an empty priced prediction (`intervened.body['downstream']['reached'] == []`), while `verbs.blast` on the identical abducted-and-intervened state reports `streaming-experience`, a real `needs` dependent, as unpriced exposure. Decision ticket 08 Q3's 'one traversal, two outputs' shown holding under an intervention, not only at rest.
  [x] 3. The intervention-aware scoring rule, with a worked example of a mitigated non-event.
        build ticket 81: twin/schema.py's `outcome` schema gains an optional `mitigation` field (build ticket 81), reusing `response.mitigates`'s own validator rather than a second one. `verbs.score` (twin/verbs.py) gates on it with the identical evidence threshold `pricing.py`'s own mitigation credit already uses (`evidence.may_price`): a non-event whose outcome carries a grade-1/2 mitigation claim is excluded from the calibration record (`unscoreable`, reason `MITIGATED_NON_EVENT`) rather than scored as a miss; the identical claim with no mitigation, or a claim graded 3-5, scores exactly as it always did — decision ticket 08 Q4's 'grades 4-5 earn NO calibration credit', made literal. The worked example is the evidence: tests/test_intervention_aware_scoring.py — one forecast bundle, three outcome records, `test_a_well_evidenced_mitigation_excludes_the_non_event_from_the_calibration_record` (grade 1, unscoreable), `test_the_identical_claim_with_no_mitigation_scores_as_an_ordinary_non_event` (scored), and `test_a_weakly_graded_mitigation_claim_earns_no_calibration_credit` (grade 4, byte-identical to the no-claim case).
  [x] 4. A stated identification/confounding discipline + its honest limits.
        build ticket 45: twin/causal_claims.py — decision ticket 08 Q5's own resolution, verbatim: declared assumptions (an unconditional reverse-causation alternative on every proposal), a MANDATORY alternative-explanation field (`propose()`'s `alternatives`, never empty — `causal_claims_over_grading_is_penalised_and_alternatives_are_mandatory` asserts it directly), and graph-based confounder flagging (`shared_ancestors()`, the detector build ticket 21 named as still-to-build and left `causal-layer` at 1/5 for). Formal identification (do-calculus, back-door analysis) is explicitly out of scope here, per Q5's own text rejecting it as a blanket requirement — so this checklist item is the STATED discipline the acceptance criterion asks for, not a change to `twin/primitives.py`'s do()/observe() semantics; `an_intervention_never_reaches_upstream`'s own note about a future backdoor-aware narrowing refers to that larger, explicitly-rejected undertaking, not this one. Honest limit stated in the module docstring: the detector walks one hop, not the full transitive closure, and a confounder the graph does not contain is invisible to it.
  [x] 5. Exercised on a real claim from each co-flagship (Qwikster→churn; EUV delay→node slip).
        build ticket 81: Netflix: `the-separation-reaches-the-streaming-service` in `fixtures.build_netflix_org` (build ticket 74), a grade-1 causal edge on the real subject, carried through propagation into a price and a trade-off curve with three rival accounts disagreeing about its magnitude. Intel (build ticket 81, closing the prior gap this file itself named): `euv-delay-slips-the-node` (`euv-lithography` -> `leading-edge-foundry-node`), a grade-2 causal edge on the real spine (`fixtures.build_intel_org`, not the walking-skeleton's `example.invalid` stand-in), dated and cited to the subject's own 2014 decision to forgo EUV at 10nm and the multi-year node slip that followed, contrasted with the subject's own current High-NA EUV bet on the identical component. Exercised live in `twin/beat-intel.sh` step 0c (`twin propagate --origin euv-lithography` composes to a priced, grade-2 elasticity — not directional-only) and asserted in tests/test_intel_beat.py::test_the_real_euv_causal_edge_composes_to_a_priced_elasticity; the sense step (0b) now finds `euv-lithography` upstream of the bound component too, the observation side of the identical edge.
==> currency-regimes: full  (6/6 of decision ticket 09)
  [x] 1. Named stakeholder(s) for the currency; multi-stakeholder decision made.
        build ticket 26: twin/schema.py `perspective` and twin/verbs.py exposure() — a perspective names who pays and which party they are, any party may declare one, and `twin exposure` with no --perspective reports every perspective rather than defaulting to the employer's.
  [x] 2. An explicit constraint class (ruin + forbidden) with schema representation and a membership rule.
        build ticket 27: twin/constraints.yaml and twin/constraints.py — two named classes, `ruin` and `forbidden`, represented in the universal floor and in the perspective schema, with the membership rule that a perspective adds to the floor and a perspective reusing a floor id does not load.
  [x] 3. The comparable-remainder boundary defined and defensible.
        build ticket 29: twin/admission.py and twin/evidence-ladder.yaml `path_admission_threshold` — an impact enters the £ only through a causal path to a cash flow the perspective declared, graded at or inside a separately published threshold. The boundary is computed from the graph, so no author can mark anything priceable, and a refused impact comes back carrying the unpriced structural blast radius rather than silence.
  [x] 4. Treatment of each named incommensurable, incl. where we refuse to price.
        build ticket 85: Six named incommensurables, six treatments. Reputation and morale price through a modelled path or stay in twin/pricing.py's register with one of five falsifiable refusal reasons (build ticket 30). Existential/tail risk gets `twin severity`'s loss-exceedance curve (VaR beside TVaR, never a point figure — build ticket 24) anchored to named public sources (build ticket 25). Ethical harms — real consequence borne by someone who never held a perspective in any twin, so could never be priced from one — was checked live rather than assumed absent: `twin/affected_parties.py` (build ticket 61, decision ticket 15's Q4 mechanism list) already existed and already refuses to price this, it was simply never stated as decision ticket 09's own sixth incommensurable. Build ticket 85 makes that connection explicit in the artefact itself: `published()`'s `currency_note` now names 'decision ticket 09 AC 4's ethical-harms leg' directly, and tests/test_affected_parties.py::test_published_names_ethical_harms_as_the_incommensurable_it_treats exercises the ethical-harms case specifically, the same standard the other five already meet. A small connection to existing machinery, not new machinery — the register already did the work; it had just never said so.
  [x] 5. A stated objective function with its qualifications.
        build ticket 33: twin/tradeoff.py curve() and twin verbs.trade_off() (twin trade-off) — the objective function decision ticket 09 Q3 names, minimise-total-net-cost-of-risk, computed as a trade-off curve across the named causal-account ensemble with a marked default rather than asserted as an answer. The default's own `basis` field states it is a default point, not a recommendation, and no_recommended_action_field-style scanning (harness guard trade_off_curve_reports_disagreement_never_a_scalar) asserts no action-shaped field or phrase exists anywhere in the body.
  [x] 6. How rival-model £ spread is reported.
        build ticket 33: twin/tradeoff.py curve() reports each admitted response's net cost of risk per named account in `curve[].net_cost_of_risk.by_account`, never averaged, plus the range between them; `agreement.cheapest_by_account` and `agreement.unanimous` surface a ranking disagreement explicitly rather than smoothing it into the default. This is the causal-account half of the spread ticket 16 already gave rival world models: `twin causal-accounts` (32) reports disagreement in a propagated influence, and this ticket carries that disagreement through pricing into a response's own comparable figure.
==> demo-slice: full  (4/4 of decision ticket 22)
  [x] 1. A single demonstrable thesis, stated in one sentence.
        build ticket 91: `twin/demo_slice.py`'s `THESIS` constant — decision ticket 22's own resolved Q1 answer, verbatim, in one place rather than distributed across comments and echo statements. `summary()` composes it into the artefact `twin demo-slice` emits; tests/test_demo_slice.py::test_summary_composes_all_four_pieces asserts the body carries it unchanged.
  [x] 2. Subject + scenario selection, with rationale.
        build ticket 91: `twin/demo_slice.py`'s `SUBJECTS` — the Royal Mail / Netflix / Intel rationale (contamination-driven for Royal Mail, whole-engine for Netflix, live-and-unresolved for Intel) collected from decision ticket 22 and build tickets 71/73/75 into the artefact's own data, not left as reference-only prose. tests/test_demo_slice.py::test_summary_composes_all_four_pieces asserts all three subjects carry a non-empty rationale.
  [x] 3. An explicit shown/stubbed/absent boundary and how it is surfaced.
        build ticket 91: `twin/demo_slice.boundary()` wires `does_not_do.published()` and `Capabilities.load()` (build ticket 77's own mechanism), filtered to `TOUCHED_CAPABILITIES` — the capabilities the three beat scripts' own artefact-producing verbs actually carry, read from their own `CAPS_*` constants rather than retyped. `shown` names them, `stubbed` is the does-not-do register's entries scoped to them, `absent` is every other loaded capability (`ethics-gate`, `forecast-book`, `honest-build`, `twin-inside-twin` today) — surfaced in the artefact's own body and printed by `twin demo-slice`, not narrated in prose a viewer has to trust. tests/test_demo_slice.py::test_boundary_shown_and_absent_partition_the_loaded_capabilities and ::test_boundary_stubbed_entries_are_scoped_to_touched_capabilities_only.
  [x] 4. Acceptance criteria for the slice, tied back to the owning tickets' criteria.
        build ticket 91: `twin/demo_slice.py`'s `ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA` — a table mapping this decision ticket's own four ACs to the build tickets that realise them (71/73/75 for subject selection, 77 for the boundary mechanism, 91 for the rendered artefact itself), emitted in the artefact body and printed by `twin demo-slice`. tests/test_demo_slice.py::test_summary_composes_all_four_pieces asserts all four indices are present, each naming at least one build ticket.
==> domain-model: full  (7/7 of decision ticket 07)
  [x] 1. A named core ontology (entity types + relationship types + the backbone) in ubiquitous language.
        build ticket 79: twin/ontology.py's entity_types()/relationship_types()/backbone(), generated from schema.py's own SCHEMAS/EDGE_TYPES/COMPONENT_KINDS/EVOLUTION rather than hand-typed — a schema change changes this artefact with no second edit. Published via `twin ontology`. tests/test_ontology.py::test_every_schema_kind_is_a_named_entity_type and test_every_edge_type_is_classified_into_exactly_one_family assert it stays a live read of schema.py rather than a snapshot.
  [x] 2. The layering decision (one graph vs coupled) with the seam(s) defined.
        build ticket 04, confirmed by build ticket 79: twin/model.py's World/Overlay split, DirectionError and enforce_direction/check_direction — the world layer may never reference an overlay, citing decision ticket 07 Q1b in the module's own docstring, built by build ticket 04. Exercised live by tests/test_seam2_model.py::test_the_world_layer_may_never_reference_an_overlay (plants a violation and asserts check_direction catches it).
  [x] 3. Edge-semantics decision incl. the structural-vs-causal distinction.
        build ticket 17: twin/schema.py EDGE_TYPES and _refine_edge — `needs` says the value chain would break, `influences` says a change here moves something there by a signed, lagged, calibrated amount. Each family refuses the other's fields on write, and the graded-edge fixture is asserted as a boundary contract.
  [x] 4. The temporal/versioning model + how scenarios are represented.
        build tickets 04 and 36, confirmed by build ticket 79: twin/repo.py's UnitRef/Pin: the world layer and each overlay are separately versioned, git-commit-pinned units — exercised by tests/test_seam2_model.py::test_an_overlay_stays_pinned_while_the_world_moves. A scenario is its own schema object (schema.py SCHEMAS['scenario']) that deliberately carries no regime; every execution must name one with no default (twin/regimes.py require()) — exercised by tests/test_regimes.py::test_a_scenario_cannot_declare_its_own_regime and test_an_execution_with_no_regime_is_refused_rather_than_defaulted.
  [x] 5. The representation/format decision (reuse vs custom) with what's authored vs derived.
        build ticket 01, confirmed by build ticket 79: twin/artefact.py's AUTHORED/DERIVED marks, enforced at attestation time by twin/attest.py (a derived artefact refuses a human signature; an authored one requires one) — exercised by tests/test_repo_and_envelope.py::test_a_derived_artefact_refuses_a_human_signature and test_an_authored_artefact_carries_the_signature_that_gives_it_accountability. What is authored vs derived is enumerated in decision ticket 07's own RESOLVED section and mirrored per module by every AUTHORED/DERIVED call site (twin/challenges.py, twin/constraints.py, twin/does_not_do.py, twin/ontology.py, ...).
  [x] 6. Where the £/risk, people, assets and signals attach to the graph.
        build ticket 79: twin/ontology.py's ATTACHMENT table names where £/risk (scenario/response/perspective), people (person nodes + maintains/knows/owns edges), assets (a component of kind 'data') and signals (signal + claim) attach, each schema_kinds entry checked against schema.py's real vocabulary by _check_attachment_vocabulary() rather than asserted in prose. tests/test_ontology.py::test_ac6_names_exactly_the_four_things_the_ticket_names and test_ac6_attachment_vocabulary_catches_a_renamed_schema_kind (the guard actually bites).
  [x] 7. Exercised against Netflix + Intel (does the model actually hold each subject?).
        build tickets 73 and 75, confirmed by build ticket 79: twin/fixtures.py's build_netflix_org and build_intel_org: full value chains of typed components (kinds capability/activity/practice/data), structural (`needs`) and causal (`influences`) edges, positioned on the evolution axis. Overlay.load's own _check_references() validates the whole ontology against both on every load — exercised by tests/test_netflix.py's netflix_spine fixture, tests/test_netflix_beat.py::test_the_dated_state_carries_no_layer_that_postdates_it (asserts the causal layer in .graph().edges appears/disappears across a rewind), and tests/test_intel_beat.py (loads the Intel overlay, its scenario and its proposition).
==> enactment: full  (5/5 of decision ticket 18)
  [x] 1. Act-vs-propose decided, with the boundary stated.
        build ticket 66: twin/enact.py exposes `propose` and nothing that disposes, asserted against an allow-list rather than a name screen (harness check `enactment_is_propose_only_at_both_layers`); twin/enact_guard.py refuses a disposing tool call at the PreToolUse boundary, so the guarantee survives the twin gaining a shell tool, an MCP GitHub server or a subagent with `gh`. The boundary is stated in the emitted artefact as two layers with the failure mode of each named — composition for the absence, a forgotten call site for the check — and the carve-out is stated with it: the twin acts freely on its own model and never on the world.
  [x] 2. A mechanism for non-IT enactment + tracking, or an explicit admission of blindness.
        build ticket 68: The criterion offers a mechanism OR an admission of blindness, and build ticket 68 supplies the mechanism decision ticket 18 Q3 chose: enactment is SENSED, through channels, with corroboration setting the evidence grade. There is no non-IT tracking pipeline, because there is no enactment pipeline at all — a declaration and a payroll run are ordinary `signal` documents bound by ordinary `claim` documents, and `verbs.sense` walks a claim that binds to a response through the same loop as one that binds to a component, emitting the same `bound-signal` artefact (harness check `enactment_is_sensed_and_corroboration_sets_the_grade`). twin/enactment-channels.yaml is a versioned, CLOSED table of six channels; the closure is what makes 'no channel is privileged' structural, because privileging one would need a field and there is none. twin/corroboration.py refuses a table where any channel prices alone, computes the grade as the strongest single-channel rung strengthened one rung per INDEPENDENT channel, and counts every subject-declared channel as one between them — so a subject cannot corroborate itself by declaring twice, and an uncorroborated self-declaration cannot reach a price-eligible grade. The non-IT case is exercised rather than described: the fixture's `raise-the-tooling-team-retention-award` is a pay lever occupying no enforcement rung, tracked to grade 2 by a declaration corroborated by a cohort-level payroll run — the verification-substrate role of decision ticket 18 Q2, with nothing enforcing it. Decision ticket 18 Q3's own guard is run rather than restated: the payroll channel is the only one observing people at all, and the table is refused at load unless twin/ethics_gate.py admits it. WHERE THIS TICK STOPS, so a reader can disagree with it knowingly. Two limits, both real. First, the CHANNEL IS DECLARED AND NOT VERIFIED: nothing here checks that the merged change, the payroll run or the counter-signature exists, so the corroboration rule holds against a mislabelling declarant only to the extent that attribution deters one — an enactment claim is refused unless `claimed_by` is a registered role, the same discipline an `override` carries, which makes a mislabelled channel somebody's to answer for rather than anonymous. Second, this tracks whether a lever was pulled and grades how well that is evidenced; nothing yet CONSUMES the graded action state — no forecast branches on it and no mitigation credit requires it — which is criterion 5 and stays unchecked.
  [x] 3. A verdict on policy-as-versioned-dependency: survives / survives-narrowed / rejected, on the risk basis.
        build ticket 66: The criterion asks for a VERDICT on the risk basis, and twin/enact.py realises that verdict rather than restating it: `--channel` is required with no default and admits exactly the two roles decision ticket 18 Q2 narrowed to — enactment channel for machine-enforceable controls, verification substrate for the levers that are not code — so a proposal cannot avoid saying which claim it is making, and the narrowing is structural rather than a sentence in a docstring. `dependency_pins()` reads the real pins the estate's separate repositories consume: six cross-repository (driftwood, ludlow and tuppence each pinning platform and nist by tag), with three self-syncs counted apart because a repository syncing itself consumes nobody's policy. WHERE THIS TICK STOPS, so a reader can disagree with it knowingly: the verdict's own rationale is that a signed pinned version makes 'this control is actually running' VERIFIABLE rather than asserted, and this code verifies neither the signature nor the force — no tag is checked, no Rekor entry looked up, no cluster asked. Both are stated as limits inside the artefact and named in build ticket 66's 'what remains'. The tick is for the verdict the criterion asks for, not for the verification the verdict argues will one day be possible.
  [x] 4. A verdict on graded enforcement and posture-as-identity on the same basis.
        build ticket 67: The criterion asks for a VERDICT, and twin/enforcement.py realises both halves of it rather than restating them. Graded enforcement SURVIVES with no special status: twin/enforcement-grades.yaml is a versioned four-rung ladder (observe, warn, constrain, block) where `block` is the bottom rung of a spectrum rather than the mechanism, a control occupies exactly one rung, and the loader refuses any number on a rung. That refusal is the verdict made structural — decision ticket 18 Q4 admitted graded enforcement because the £ engine ALREADY prices partial mitigation through the control's own evidence-graded `mitigates` claim, so a reduction per rung would be a free multiplier: tighten the rung, earn more credit, evidence nothing. The harness check `enforcement_is_a_spectrum_and_never_prices_a_rung` asserts that the same control produces an identical `Option` at every rung, which is the only object the pre-filter accepts and therefore the only thing that can reach a price. Posture-as-identity SURVIVES NARROWED, and the narrowing is computed rather than declared: a control qualifies only if its rung changes the outcome and its posture is stamped by something that is not the subject, and there is no field an author can set to claim it. Five unsupported cases are named and published as exclusions. Moving a control between rungs is versioned like an evidence grade — an `enforcement_moves` record, a contiguous chain ending where the control stands, and a git-history check at `twin validate` that catches the first unrecorded move. WHERE THIS TICK STOPS: `signed` attaches to the authored posture artefact and to each move's registered role binding, NOT to the git commit that carried the move — this repository's commits are not keyless-signed, which estate/verify/provenance/verify-provenance.sh already records and build ticket 66 already reported against the dependency pins. The ladder's `warn` rung has no realisation in this estate and says so in its own entry.
  [x] 5. The action-state feedback path that closes ticket 08's conditional-forecast loop.
        build ticket 86: Build ticket 68 built the read side; build ticket 86 built the CONSUMER, at exactly the join build ticket 68's own AC 2 evidence named as open: `twin/pricing.py`'s `_credit()` computed mitigation credit purely from a response's own `mitigates` claim and its evidence grade, never calling `corroboration.state()` — confirmed by grep before this ticket, zero hits. `_credit()` now calls it, against the OPTION the claim credits rather than the component it reduces: a claim evidenced well enough to price is still refused — named `pricing.NOT_ENACTED`, distinct from `pricing.CLAIM_TOO_WEAK` — if that option carries no corroborated enactment. Two different causal assertions, checked separately: 'this removes part of the impact' (the claim's own evidence grade) and 'this was actually done' (`corroboration.state()`), so 'the incident did not happen because of our control' can no longer be asserted on an evidenced-but-unenacted control. Threaded through both call sites that reach `_credit()` — `twin/verbs.py`'s `price()` and `twin/tradeoff.py`'s `curve()` — with `overlay` a required parameter of `pricing.price()` rather than a default-able one, because a parameter free to be omitted would be a silent way to skip the gate. Worked example, the criterion's own literal ask: identical claims, identical priced impact, one option corroborated as enacted and one not, scoring differently — `tests/test_pricing.py::test_identical_claims_score_differently_by_corroborated_enactment`, reusing build ticket 68's own `pin-the-tooling-image-set` (corroborated) and `report-node-schedule-variance` (self-declared only) fixture cases so the split is attributable to nothing but the enactment state. Harness guard `mitigation_credit_is_gated_on_corroborated_enactment_not_just_claimed_evidence` mirrors `enforcement_is_a_spectrum_and_never_prices_a_rung`'s shape: the same differential at the unit level, plus the live `verbs.price()` artefact path on the pocket-org fixture, first crediting `retrain-the-on-call-rota` against its own (newly added) corroborating enactment claims and then refusing the identical claim the moment those two claims are stripped from an otherwise-identical fixture. WHERE THIS TICK STOPS, so a reader can disagree with it knowingly: an enactment is still evidence about no PROPOSITION or world model — `Overlay.forecast_subject` still returns `None` for it, unchanged by this ticket — so a forecast still does not branch on action state; only mitigation credit consumes it. And the channel-declared-not-verified limit build ticket 68's own AC 2 evidence already named is unchanged and unclosed by this ticket: gating credit on a declared channel still trusts the declaration, not an independent check that the merged change or payroll run exists.
==> ethics-gate: full  (5/5 of decision ticket 15)
  [x] 1. A sensor admission rule reconciling total-scope ambition with minimisation.
        build ticket 47: twin/ethics_gate.py walk_ladder(): purpose, then necessity, then proportionality, walked in order with a recorded justification per rung, stopping at the first failing rung — harness guard ethics_gate_ladder_stops_early_and_fast_improvement_is_never_an_automatic_finding proves the later rungs are never evaluated once an earlier one fails, by handing them a payload that would raise if ever read. Decision ticket 15 Q1's ladder, mechanised rather than only decided in prose.
  [x] 2. The sensor set + granularity decision.
        build ticket 82: twin/sensors.yaml (build ticket 82): a versioned, closed table naming six sensors, what each observes, and its coarsest-safe granularity (structural/behavioural x aggregate/cohort/ individual, decision ticket 15 Q1 rung 2's own vocabulary), mirroring enactment-channels.yaml's shape. twin/ethics_gate.py load_sensors() refuses an entry missing a declared kind or granularity (tests/test_ethics_gate.py::test_load_sensors_refuses_an_entry_missing_a_declared_granularity), and admit() now refuses a payload whose sensor id is not a row in that table (test_admit_refuses_an_unnamed_sensor_id) rather than accepting any string — the exact hole this AC named. Every registered id is one a real caller already proposes: the five sensors ethics_gate.labelled_corpus() evaluates against, plus payroll-record, the one enactment channel enactment-channels.yaml declares observes people.
  [x] 3. A stated Goodhart position with the most-gameable sensors named.
        build ticket 47: twin/ethics_gate.py classify_gameability() and prefer(): a sensor is marked goodhart-proof (gaming the metric requires doing the genuinely desired thing) or marked (the safe default — cheaply gamed for no useful effect), and prefer() records which candidate the preference rule chose, and why. flag_fast_improvement()/adjudicate_fast_improvement() build the backstop (Q2c): a fast-improving metric raises a flag whose own output carries no action/verdict-shaped field (checked against the same banned-word scan no_recommended_action_field runs), and only a human holding a registered role (twin/roles.yaml) can adjudicate it into an actual finding. Decision ticket 15 Q2 already named the position and its worked examples (bus-factor vs. commit counts/message sentiment/hours-online) in prose; this ticket is the first to give it a mechanism.
  [x] 4. A named misuse catalogue, each with its blocking constraint.
        build ticket 82: twin/behavioural-misuse-catalogue.yaml (build ticket 82): the eight named misuses of decision ticket 15's own Q3 table — suppressing pay, justifying layoffs, surveillance creep, performance management by proxy, blame attribution after an incident, detecting union organising, decision laundering, weaponising another org's twin — each entry naming a mechanism, not just a risk, cross-referenced against twin/constraints.yaml's universal-floor ids (no-automatic-enactment, no-individual-level-output, no-individual-attribution, no-special-category-representation) and twin/ethics_gate.py's ladder rungs (_check_purpose(), admit()'s sensor-table check). Loaded through twin/misuse.py's own load_catalogue(), pointed at BEHAVIOURAL_CATALOGUE_PATH rather than a second loader — tests/test_misuse.py::test_the_behavioural_catalogue_loads_through_the_same_loader_and_names_a_mechanism_each, ::test_the_two_catalogues_do_not_conflate_their_scopes proves no id or subject overlap with build ticket 62's governance-scoped twin/misuse-catalogue.yaml.
  [x] 5. The operational gate mechanism, incl. what detaching the overlay means.
        build ticket 47: Two halves, matching decision ticket 15's own resolution verbatim. Detachment: build ticket 07's behavioural overlay is a separately-gated store, a demonstrable act, unchanged by this ticket. Admission: twin/ethics_gate.py admit() combines walk_ladder() with dpia_triage() — a sensor is admitted only when the ladder passes AND, where a DPIA is mandatory under the ICO's 2023 monitoring guidance (email/message/keystroke/biometric monitoring, profiling, or financial-loss risk to the worker — research 05 Part B.2), the payload records dpia.complete. tests/test_ethics_gate.py::test_admit_refuses_when_dpia_mandatory_and_not_complete_even_though_ladder_passes demonstrates the ladder and the DPIA gate operating as two distinct checks, not one.
==> forecast-book: full  (6/6 of decision ticket 21)
  [x] 1. A question-selection rule that does not reintroduce selection bias.
        build ticket 57: twin/benchmark-selection-rule.yaml — versioned, committed and stated in resolvable terms (liquidity threshold, resolution horizon, category list); twin/benchmark.py's select_questions() applies it mechanically (sorted by id, no judgement call) and reproducibly (the identical rule against the identical pool always draws the identical set), with a deterministic seeded volume valve for when the mechanical filter selects too many. Selected sets are required to span every declared confidence bin, checked against the emitted distribution rather than asserted.
  [x] 2. Venue decision + the observe-vs-participate call (incl. UK legality).
        build ticket 58: Decided at decision ticket 21 Q4: observe only, never participate (no UK gambling exposure; play-money forecasts land within 1-5pp of real-money ones, so money-backing buys no epistemic gain). Made structural, not merely stated: twin/forecast_book.py exposes exactly `emit`, `score_resolution`, `is_blind` — no function that takes a stake, a side or an order — asserted as an allow-list by harness guard `forecast_book_is_blind_by_construction_and_observe_only` (the same discipline `prefilter_precedes_pricing` uses on twin/options.py, so a differently-named position-placing function would still be caught), and every emitted forecast-emission body carries `observe_only: true, position_placed: false`.
  [x] 3. A blind-emission protocol that is mechanically enforceable.
        build ticket 58: twin/forecast_book.py's emit() refuses to build an emission artefact timed at or after its question's own declared resolution_window_opens_at — checked at the boundary and past it, not merely on obviously-late input — via is_blind(), the identical function an auditor re-runs later against nothing but the artefact's own recorded body. Signed and pinned through the existing machinery, reused rather than reinvented: emit() returns a `derived` Artefact, which attest.build() agent-signs and refuses a human signature on (derived_never_human_signed), so 'we forecast before we looked' is a provable claim, not an assurance.
  [x] 4. An explicit claim-scope statement: what a good score proves and what it does not.
        build ticket 58: twin/forecast_book.py's CLAIM_SCOPE constant, carried in the body of every forecast-emission and resolution-score artefact this module emits (published with every result, not stated once in prose): `evidences` names non-overconfidence in general world-forecasting on a pre-registered, blind, co-registered question set; `does_not_evidence` names Wardley propagation, the causal elasticities, £ pricing, the org-specific overlay and anything at organisational scale explicitly; `residual_limit` states decision ticket 21 Q1's own honesty condition — the quarantine proves no direct ingestion, not that priors were unshaped by market-adjacent information arriving some other way.
  [x] 5. The circularity question resolved (signal source vs benchmark).
        build ticket 59: twin/market_signals.py's market_signal_run() is the 'signal source' half decision ticket 21 Q1(b) names — price MOVES from a non-quarantined market feed signal-classify freely, exactly as Q1(b) decided — proactively excluding a quarantined question id before classification ever runs, not only auditable after the fact. The 'benchmark' half is build ticket 57's quarantine (twin/benchmark.py). The two are asserted together, not separately: the harness guard price_levels_never_probabilities runs 57's own audit_quarantine() over 59's live ingestion-provenance output and requires a clean result, so 'signal source vs benchmark' is one demonstrated pipeline rather than two mechanisms that happen to agree today. Levels never masquerade as probabilities either: as_probability() refuses unconditionally (price_levels_never_probabilities, the invariant), and no emitted artefact carries a probability-shaped field.
  [x] 6. A proportionality verdict: is it worth building at this coverage?
        build ticket 84: The criterion asks for a VERDICT, and twin/benchmark.py's proportionality_verdict() realises it rather than restating decision ticket 21 Q3's own resolved prose. Every number the verdict is checked against is read off what is actually delivered, not an aspiration: question_count and spans_full_confidence_range come from the real BenchmarkSet select_questions() draws against the live twin/benchmark-selection-rule.yaml (tests/test_benchmark.py::test_the_committed_rule_yields_a_yes_verdict_against_a_spanning_pool exercises the committed rule directly, not a fixture rule); capability_share is computed from len(list(caps)) — the live count of twin/capabilities/*.yaml, not a hardcoded fraction; resolution_cadence reads real score_resolution() output when supplied and honestly states 'not yet a measured one' against the pre-registered horizon window when it is not (this suite reaches no live venue, per twin/market_signals.py's own admission). The verdict is exactly one of yes/no/conditional, each earned by a structural fact rather than asserted: 'no' when the delivered set is empty, 'conditional' when it is non-empty but fails the rule's own spans_full_confidence_range() bar, 'yes' only when both hold — at which point decision ticket 21 Q3's own cited cost (MARGINAL_COST: three already-built components, tickets 57-59, layered on the ticket-20 scoring harness) and value (DISPROPORTIONATE_VALUE: the only contamination-proof falsification mechanism in the project) are cited into the artefact verbatim. tests/test_benchmark.py covers all three branches plus the cadence and capability-share computations (test_verdict_is_yes_when_the_delivered_set_spans_every_bin, test_verdict_is_conditional_when_the_delivered_set_does_not_span_every_bin, test_verdict_is_no_when_the_delivered_set_is_empty, test_verdict_reports_cadence_as_designed_not_measured_with_no_resolutions, test_verdict_reports_a_measured_cadence_from_real_resolutions, test_verdict_capability_share_is_computed_against_the_live_capability_count). WHERE THIS TICK STOPS: the verdict's cost/value framing is decision ticket 21 Q3's own resolved reasoning, cited rather than re-derived from first principles — this artefact makes that reasoning checkable against live delivered numbers, it does not re-litigate whether the reasoning itself is correct.
