==> scenario-engine: full  (7/7 of decision ticket 13)
  [x] 1. The scenario object defined, incl. its references and its relationship to forecasts.
        build ticket 06: twin/verbs.py run() — a scenario references {components, graph version, world models, time} and emits forecasts as a list, always, one per named world model.
  [x] 2. Concrete semantics for fast-forward, rewind and play, each distinguished.
        build ticket 37: twin/verbs.py run() is fast-forward — projection with no intervention, demonstrated in tests/test_four_verbs.py::test_projection_is_run_with_no_intervention. twin/primitives.py rewind() is the time half of rewind (build ticket 35) and twin/regimes.py the information-gate half (build ticket 36). twin/primitives.py Do is play (build ticket 22). Build ticket 37 composes the two primitives into all four operations — projection alone, do() alone (act-now), rewind+do() (the counterfactual), rewind+run() (the backtest, `twin backtest`) — with no backtest-specific code path: harness guard backtest_is_a_pure_composition asserts an explicit rewind+run computes the identical forecast run() alone computes at the same time, differing only in which command produced it.
  [x] 3. Where scenarios live + how they are versioned/diffed.
        build ticket 88: Git already supplied the storage/versioning half (build ticket 06); the gap was the renderer. twin/scenario_diff.py's diff() (build ticket 88) takes a scenario id and two git refs — branch-per-scenario (research 03) or two commits on one branch alike, since ModelRepo.open(path, ref=...) already opens either — and reports a field-level diff of the scenario itself (question, proposition, at, horizon, components, world_models) plus a map-diff of every component either side's overlay places (Graph.wardley(), no separate authoring step). Wired as `twin scenario-diff` (twin/cli.py cmd_scenario_diff). tests/test_scenario_diff.py exercises a real repository with a real `explore` branch: a scalar field change and a component's evolution-stage regression both reported, an unchanged field/component not reported, identical refs diffing to nothing, a scenario authored on only one side reported via `scenario_present` rather than refused (mid-flight branch-per-scenario authoring is a real state), and absence at both sides refused.
  [x] 4. How opportunity/gameplay moves are proposed, with the negativity counterweight addressed.
        build ticket 46: twin/gameplay_lens.py propose() checks a two-play catalogue (land-grab, exploit-commoditisation) against the current map's evolution positions, needs-edge dependency structure and ownership, with the reason and the checked preconditions attached per opportunity. gameplay_lens.sweep() runs it unconditionally across every org overlay of every named repository, no human naming a component — the structural pull against the record's push (decision ticket 13 Q3) — and reports opportunity-candidate volume beside each org's bound-signal volume in the same artefact so the counterweight is measurable.
  [x] 5. Admissibility rule for the scenario library.
        build ticket 69: twin/schema.py's closed COMMITTED_SCENARIO_CLASSES enum and the optional scenario.class field make "belongs to the standing library" mechanical: a scenario is admissible by carrying one of the nine committed classes (or by being unconditionally swept per build ticket 09 — decision ticket 13's other admissibility arm). fixtures.build_library_org populates all nine; harness guard standing_library_covers_committed_classes (twin/invariants/checks.py) fails CI the moment one is silently dropped, demonstrated in tests/test_scenario_library.py::test_dropping_a_committed_class_from_the_library_fails_the_invariant. The full "preconditions/triggers checkable against the map" reading of admissibility — the precondition-triggered arm — stays for build ticket 46 (gameplay-lens); this ticket closes only the committed-set arm.
  [x] 6. A selection/prioritisation rule for which scenarios run (the combinatorics answer).
        build ticket 88: The four tiers decision ticket 13 names — standing library, precondition-triggered, event-triggered, ad-hoc — are proven the complete, non-overlapping set by checking the source directly (build ticket 88), the same discipline harness guard `backtest_is_a_pure_composition` already uses for a structural CLI-command claim. tests/test_scenario_selection_tiers.py: exactly two primitives can produce a scenario execution or an opportunity — verbs.run() and gameplay_lens.propose()/.sweep() — and every call site of either across twin/*.py is one of schedule.py (tier 1, standing library, unconditional loop over every scenario in every overlay — no scenario named), gameplay_lens.py (tier 2, unconditional map scan — no component named), or cli.py's cmd_run/cmd_backtest (tiers 3 and 4, event-triggered and ad-hoc, sharing the identical call because decision ticket 13 distinguishes them only by why a human or automation invoked it, not by a different code path); reproduce.py's replay() also calls verbs.run() but originates nothing — it reads the scenario id out of an already-emitted artefact's own recorded command, never a fresh argument. A fifth caller of either primitive anywhere in twin/*.py would fail the test.
  [x] 7. Exercised on one fear scenario and one opportunity scenario across the co-flagships.
        build ticket 88: Netflix already had both (build ticket 74): the fear scenario rewound under `as-consumed` (`twin backtest`) and the opportunity a `land-grab` on `streaming-service` pulled by `twin gameplay-sweep`. Intel had only its fear scenario on the real spine (`does-the-14a-bet-land-a-named-customer`, build ticket 75); its opportunity side existed only as the toy `euv-slip-2026` fixture citing `example.invalid`, unrelated to the real spine. Build ticket 88 adds `euv-readiness-wins-the-14a-opportunity` to `fixtures.build_intel_org` — the real, cited EUV causal edge build ticket 81 added (`euv-lithography` -> `leading-edge-foundry-node`), read the upside way: does the subject's own High-NA EUV tooling readiness hold through its own named 2026 go/no-go, positioning it to win the external customer commitment, the reverse of the historical slip the same edge's own note cites. Named honestly rather than forced into the identical shape Netflix's own opportunity took: `gameplay_lens.propose()` finds nothing to pull on this org's overlay (no person edges, no component near the product/commodity band — checked live, not assumed), so the opportunity is authored as a scenario on the same real edge and world models the fear scenario already uses, not pulled by the sweep. Exercised live in `twin/beat-intel.sh` steps 3a/3b (both scenarios run standalone and inside `twin sweep`, byte-identical, plural distinct forecasts each) and asserted in tests/test_intel_beat.py::test_the_opportunity_scenario_is_framed_as_a_win_not_a_threat, ::test_the_opportunity_scenario_is_built_on_the_real_euv_edge_not_a_toy_one (no `example.invalid` anywhere in the emitted artefact), ::test_the_opportunity_forecast_is_plural_and_the_ensemble_genuinely_disagrees, and ::test_no_outcome_is_authored_for_the_opportunity_scenario_either — the same permanent honesty as the fear scenario, since this org's story has not happened yet either way. The toy `euv-slip-2026` fixture in the walking-skeleton `intel` overlay (`fixtures.build()`, distinct from `build_intel_org`) is left as-is: it was never the real spine and this criterion was never asking for that fixture to change, only for the real spine to stop lacking an opportunity side.
==> sense-move: full  (8/8 of decision ticket 11)
  [x] 1. A signal object defined in ubiquitous language, with provenance + STEEP class.
        build ticket 05: twin/verbs.py sense() plus the fixture signals — a dated, STEEP-tagged, provenanced signal binds to a component through a committed grade-5 claim and emits a bound-signal artefact.
  [x] 2. The binding mechanism decided, incl. what is automated vs judged vs reviewed.
        build ticket 43: twin/signal_classify.py: binding is automated at volume through the seam-3 skill harness (twin/skills.py, build ticket 42), evaluated against the pooled Carillion/NMC/Wirecard/Enron labelled corpus with a declared threshold (twin/skill-thresholds.yaml). Automated output is grade 5 by construction (no parameter can set it higher, checked structurally by the harness guard signal_classify_is_grade_5_by_construction) and stays contestable (build ticket 60) rather than gated at ingestion — decision ticket 11 Q2's 'judged' and 'reviewed' halves.
  [x] 3. Authored-vs-inferred position decided, consistent with ticket 07's authored/derived split.
        build ticket 44: twin/evolution_judge.py: position is inferred first from accumulated evidence (judge(), grade 5 by construction) and a human may then correct it (override(), grade 4, attributable to a registered role) — decision ticket 11 Q1's inferred-first order, structurally enforced (override() cannot run without judge()'s own output as its first argument). The twin never goes silent: pushback() always states its basis, agreement included. Extends twin/schema.py's claim schema (kind 'position'/'override', evolution_position field) and the invariant suite (evolution_judge_output_is_graded_by_construction_and_never_silent).
  [x] 4. Observation-propagation semantics, distinguished from ticket 08's intervention propagation.
        build ticket 80: twin/verbs.py sense() (build ticket 80) now runs propagate_mod.propagate() (downstream) and primitives_mod.updated_beliefs(graph, Observe(component)) (upstream, ticket 22/35's own primitive) on every component-kind binding, publishing both under a propagation field on the bound-signal artefact's binding. tests/test_primitives.py::test_sense_reaches_an_ancestor_the_same_component_intervention_would_not exercises it against the real fixture data sense() already binds (price-separation-announced -> dvd-by-mail): sensing reaches the causal ancestors streaming-experience and content-delivery-network; intervene() on the identical component, over the identical edges, reaches neither — the distinction from ticket 08's do()/severed() (build ticket 22) made concrete against real data, not asserted only against the bare primitives.
  [x] 5. Weak-signal retention + promotion rule.
        build ticket 55: Conjunctive, and both halves now exist. Retention: twin/unbound_pool.py (build ticket 54) reads every unbound signal, weighs it against a published decay function (twin/decay.yaml) and reports it, decayed or not, never dropped. Promotion: twin/retrospective_sweep.py (build ticket 55) re-examines every unbound signal against the current model's candidate components (twin/ingest.py's candidates_of(), reused) and rebinds one a model change makes interpretable, gated on signal_classify.best_match()'s own score so a rebind is genuine rather than signal_classify's own tie-broken default — a signal past the decay threshold is rescued exactly the same as a live one (harness guard retrospective_sweep_rescues_a_decayed_signal_when_a_model_change_binds_it), and each rebound signal carries a computed lead_time_to_recognition_days from pool-entry date to binding date, reported as a first-class body field rather than an internal statistic.
  [x] 6. A stated position on sensor gameability.
        build ticket 47: twin/ethics_gate.py (build ticket 47, decision ticket 15 Q2): classify_gameability() marks a sensor goodhart-proof only on positive evidence that gaming its metric requires doing the genuinely desired thing, defaulting to marked otherwise; prefer() applies that as a preference rule among candidates and records the choice; flag_fast_improvement()/adjudicate_fast_improvement() build the backstop — a fast-improving metric is grounds for suspicion, never an automatic finding, and only a human holding a registered role can adjudicate one. The same code ticks ethics-gate AC 3 (decision ticket 15) — sensor gameability is the genuine overlap between sensing (this capability) and its ethics gate.
  [x] 7. The loop's cadence + re-price triggers, sufficient to generate forecast volume.
        build ticket 09: twin/schedule.py sweep() is the scheduled half — every scenario in every org overlay of every named repository, run unconditionally, with no staleness skip (harness guard scheduled_emission_ignores_signal_presence proves two runs over an unchanged repository emit the same count). twin run --scenario X, unchanged since build ticket 06, is the event-driven half decision ticket 11 Q5 asks to sit beside it.
  [x] 8. Exercised on a real signal for each co-flagship.
        build ticket 80: twin/beat-netflix.sh step 0b runs twin sense against q4-2011-letter-2012-01-25, the real Netflix checkpoint (build ticket 73's own spine) whose late arrival first supplies the causal edge dvd-by-mail -> streaming-service, and prints the observation reaching dvd-by-mail upstream; twin/beat-intel.sh step 0b runs twin sense against tan-14a-customer-guidance-2026-01-23, a real Intel checkpoint (build ticket 75's own spine), and prints the (honestly empty, that org's causal layer does not exist yet) reach. Both binding claims needed a binding_grade override (fixtures.py NETFLIX_SENSE_DEMO_SIGNAL / INTEL_SENSE_DEMO_SIGNAL, build ticket 80) to reach the grade 5 sense() requires — the other Netflix and Intel checkpoints' binding claims stay coupled to their signal's own sourcing grade, a named, out-of-scope gap (ticket 80's own 'what still isn't true'). Both beat scripts run to PASS end to end.
==> synthetic-substrate: full  (7/7 of decision ticket 12)
  [x] 1. The real/synthetic seam defined, with a consistency rule between spine and substrate.
        build ticket 50: twin/spine.py — the spine is the org's own real, dated `signal` documents (Spine.from_overlay reads twin/model.py's Overlay.signals directly, no second authored format), and the consistency rule is enforced rather than declared: reconcile() refuses, naming what is missing, if the substrate does not carry every spine fact knowable by a checkpoint (anchor()), verbatim; reconcile_at_every_checkpoint() runs that check once per distinct spine date. diff_against_spine() demonstrates the free-running half — everything not anchored — stays wide on the real Carillion fixture, so a planted signal sits among dozens of non-plant decoys rather than standing alone as the diff's sole residual (the failure decision ticket 12 Q3 names as 'actively dangerous'), while an over-anchored negative control does expose the plant as the sole residual, proving the guard measures a real property.
  [x] 2. A fidelity target + a stated list of what would make the substrate an unfair test.
        build ticket 51: twin/substrate_eval.py — five declared, targeted dimensions (signal_to_noise, plant_difficulty, spine_consistency, reporting_asymmetry, mundanity), each a real value computed from a batch's own content, not a manual eyeball: evaluate_fidelity() and TARGETS give every dimension a target band, and UNFAIR_TEST_CONDITIONS states what would make the substrate an unfair test, matching decision ticket 12's own list — over-anchored noise (caught by build ticket 50's diff_against_spine), silent drift, a trivially findable plant, a falsely balanced positive/negative split, and volume too thin to bury a plant — each demonstrated failing its own dimension on a real, deliberately-constructed batch (tests/test_substrate_eval.py), not merely asserted plausible. tune() is the supported tuning loop AC asks for: a balanced 50/50 template mix genuinely misses the reporting-asymmetry target, and the loop converges over more than one iteration to a batch that clears every band, negativity bias included (decision ticket 12 Q3c, spec story 60 — reporting asymmetry as measured and negativity bias as produced are the same property).
  [x] 3. The planting protocol (strength, lead time, burial, distribution of difficulty).
        build ticket 87: twin/planter.py's Plant.strength (a declared unit-interval float, read from twin/plant-horizons.yaml beside the horizon and reason it already carries, enforced with the identical 'every plant must carry one' discipline — plant() refuses a recipe whose planted signals are not every one covered by a strength, tests/test_planter.py::test_plant_refuses_a_planted_signal_with_no_declared_strength) closes the one field this criterion had no home for; lead time (actionability_horizon) and burial (plant_difficulty) were already ticked by build tickets 73 and 51. Distribution of difficulty is twin/substrate_eval.py's new plant_difficulty_spread(): the max-minus-min across a batch's own per-plant difficulty scores, added to TARGETS/evaluate_fidelity/UNFAIR_TEST_CONDITIONS beside the existing mean-based plant_difficulty rather than replacing it — a batch where every plant sits at the identical difficulty now genuinely fails a real, constructed negative control (tests/test_substrate_eval.py::test_a_uniform_difficulty_batch_fails_plant_difficulty_spread, using UNCAMOUFLAGED_PLANTED_SIGNALS, spread == 0.0) while the camouflaged PLANTED_SIGNALS batch and the real committed Netflix substrate both show a genuine spread (0.333 and 0.6 respectively, test_the_tuned_batchs_plants_show_a_genuine_difficulty_spread, tests/test_netflix.py::test_every_fidelity_dimension_lands_inside_its_band).
  [x] 4. A blind/adversarial separation mechanism between planter and detector.
        build ticket 52: twin/planter.py, twin/detector.py, twin/scorer.py — a real, enforced adversarial split, not a promise: the planter (twin/planter.py) is the only module that ever reads substrate_generator.generate()'s plants field, and hands the detector only PlantedWorld.public (the batch with plants stripped); the detector (twin/detector.py) imports nothing naming planter (checked by AST scan of its real source, tests/test_detector.py::test_detector_module_imports_nothing_naming_planter) and is behaviourally blind, not merely unwired — detect() returns byte-identical output whether or not a decoy plants key is spliced into its input (test_detect_is_indifferent_to_a_spliced_in_ground_truth_key), so ground truth cannot leak back in even if a caller tried; the scorer (twin/scorer.py) reads both as two independent arguments (ground_truth, detections — never a merged object). The limit decision ticket 12 Q2 names is recorded, not papered over: every ScoreResult carries SHARED_PRIOR_LIMITATION verbatim (planter and detector share model priors; a synthetic result is never evidence the twin anticipates the world). Every plant also carries an actionability horizon (Plant.actionability_horizon): a detection on or before it scores TIMELY_SCORE, one after scores LATE_SCORE — near-zero, not zero — with the horizon named in the reason string, and a missed plant scores MISSED_SCORE (tests/test_scorer.py; harness guard planter_detector_scorer_are_structurally_separated_and_late_detection_scores_near_zero).
  [x] 5. Generation method + reproducibility/versioning decision.
